  "narrative_body": "Pemex spent eight weeks blaming a ghost tanker for a spill that fouled 900 kilometres of Gulf coastline before admitting, under pressure, that a ruptured company pipeline near the Cantarell complex was responsible all along. Three officials were fired. The damage extends well beyond the oil.

The concealment cuts against Sheinbaum's most carefully cultivated credential. She came to office as an environmental engineer and climate scientist. When Oceana called the spill an act of ecocide and demanded criminal accountability — and when opposition legislators pressed for full disclosure of spill volumes and remediation costs — the pressure came not from political opponents but from an environmental civil society that otherwise supports her. Three dismissals and a Gulf monitoring observatory do not answer that charge. A second, unrelated hydrocarbon leak at an abandoned well in Minatitlán, Veracruz, reported in the same week, deepened the picture of institutional decay at an oil company already carrying over $100 billion in debt.

Even so, the week's diplomatic headline belonged elsewhere. In Barcelona, Sheinbaum held a private hour-long meeting with Pedro Sánchez, Spain's prime minister, formally ending an eight-year freeze. She declared there had been no diplomatic crisis — offering face-saving cover for both sides — and invited Mr Sánchez to host the next Summit in Defense of Democracy in Mexico in 2027. The rapprochement was made possible by King Felipe VI's March acknowledgment of the colonial "abuse" of indigenous peoples, a step short of the formal apology Andrés Manuel López Obrador had demanded but sufficient for Sheinbaum's framing. Spain's economy minister moved quickly, signalling interest in energy, infrastructure, and financial sectors.

The summit produced something more unexpected. A joint communiqué signed by Sheinbaum, Mr Sánchez, and Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva, Brazil's president, called on the Cuban government to allow its people to decide their future "in full freedom," citing democratic values and human rights. Mr López Obrador applied the Estrada Doctrine rigidly throughout his presidency, shielding Cuba from any external pressure as a matter of non-intervention principle. Sheinbaum did not reverse that policy, but she signed language her predecessor never would have — positioning Mexico within a progressive-democratic multilateral framing rather than the pure sovereignty camp.

During the visit, Sheinbaum also toured the Barcelona Supercomputing Center and announced a collaboration on Mexico's own Coatlicue supercomputer project. It is the first concrete technology cooperation announcement on the EU track, and a deliberate signal that Mexico's digital infrastructure investment need not run exclusively through Washington.

The broader EU relationship gained institutional depth at home. Laura Itzel Castillo, the Senate president, met with the EU's delegation chief and committed the Senate to accompanying ratification of the pending EU-Mexico Global Modernised Agreement, citing €82 billion in annual goods trade and €209 billion in accumulated EU investment. Separately, Marcelo Ebrard, the economy secretary, met with the national business council to coordinate positions ahead of the second round of Mexico-US trade review talks the following Monday. Both tracks running in parallel is the point: Mexico is advancing the EU relationship without letting the US anchor slip.

The domestic legislative pace matched the diplomatic one. Sheinbaum signed a decree creating the Servicio Universal de Salud, integrating the two main federal health systems and all 32 state health services into a single universal free healthcare guarantee — the most significant change to Mexico's health architecture in decades. The Senate approved a constitutional reform granting Congress authority to standardise feminicide law across all 32 states; the vote was 109-0, a genuine cross-party consensus unusual in this legislature. A new cinema and audiovisual law brought streaming platforms including Netflix under domestic content quotas. The Infrastructure Investment Law, published earlier in the month, provided the statutory backbone for the Plan México investment model.

Quietly, on the security side, the US Treasury's sanctions office designated six individuals and entities linked to the Cártel del Noreste for running gambling operations in Nuevo Laredo and Tampico. Mexico's financial intelligence unit filed criminal complaints and co-ordinated the closure of three casino operators in parallel. The operation drew little notice — which is, in a way, the point. Formal bilateral security frameworks are not in active use; this kind of working-level financial crime cooperation is what fills the gap.",
